WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.880
File handling and process execution are closely connected because many
child processes read input files, write output files, inspect directories,
or create temporary artifacts.

2
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:19.631
A safe design builds paths from trusted base directories, uses path-aware
APIs, validates identifiers, and checks that resolved file access remains
inside approved locations.

3
00:00:19.631 --> 00:00:26.993
User-facing display names should be separated from storage names so a
friendly label does not become an instruction to the filesystem.

4
00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:32.869
Working directory control matters because relative paths are interpreted
relative to a current location.

5
00:00:32.869 --> 00:00:41.199
If the working directory is inherited accidentally or controlled
indirectly, the same argument may point at different files in different
deployments.

6
00:00:41.199 --> 00:00:45.655
A secure design sets the working directory intentionally or avoids relying
on it.

7
00:00:45.655 --> 00:00:52.758
The process should only see the files and directories needed for the
approved operation, not broad application or host locations.

8
00:00:52.758 --> 00:01:00.314
Temporary files deserve care because they often hold uploads, transformed
data, reports, exports, or intermediate processing results.

9
00:01:00.314 --> 00:01:09.031
Predictable temporary names, shared directories, weak permissions,
incomplete cleanup, and reuse across concurrent jobs can create exposure
or confusion.

10
00:01:09.031 --> 00:01:19.815
Safer patterns use platform helpers that create unpredictable names with
appropriate permissions, keep temporary files in approved locations, and
clean up during both success and failure paths.

11
00:01:19.815 --> 00:01:23.948
Permissions on input and output files should match the risk of the
workflow.

12
00:01:23.948 --> 00:01:33.506
A child process should not need access to source code, production
configuration, private keys, unrelated customer data, or broad system
directories to perform a narrow task.

13
00:01:33.506 --> 00:01:39.124
File access should be explicit enough that code review can identify what
the process can read and write.

14
00:01:39.124 --> 00:01:45.000
This is one reason process execution should be treated as an architecture
decision, not just a utility call.
